27</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1098/rstb.2007.0033">doi:10.1098/rstb.2007.0033</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18267900</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sawyer</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Climate change, biofuels and eco-social impacts in the Brazilian Amazon and Cerrado</article-title><source>Phil. Trans. R. Soc. B</source><volume>363</volume><year>2008</year><fpage>1747</fpage><lpage>1752</lpage><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1098/rstb.2007.0030">doi:10.1098/rstb.2007.0030</ext-link><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18267903</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><title>Endnote</title><fn id="fn1"><label>1</label><p>There is some confusion in usage of the acronym REDD. It is most commonly used as an abbreviation for Reduced Emissions from Deforestation and Degradation. Official UNFCCC documentation tends to refer to Reduced Emissions from Deforestation in developing countries, but abbreviates this to RED-dc, not REDD. The acronym RED is also used when only deforestation is discussed and not degradation, as in some papers in this volume.</p></fn></fn-group><fn-group><fn><p>One contribution of 27 to a Theme Issue &#x02018;Climate change and the fate of the Amazon&#x02019;.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>